---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TimelineItem from "../components/TimelineItem.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="About — Adam Richie-Halford"
  description="Background and experience of Adam Richie-Halford, technology leader at Stanford University."
>
  <section class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6 py-24 sm:py-32">
    <div class="max-w-3xl">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 mb-8">
        <picture>
          <source srcset="/images/headshot.webp" type="image/webp" />
          <img
            src="/images/headshot.jpg"
            alt="Adam Richie-Halford"
            width="80"
            height="80"
            loading="eager"
            class="w-20 h-20 rounded-full object-cover ring-2 ring-slate-800 ring-offset-2 ring-offset-slate-950 flex-shrink-0 scale-110"
            style="object-position: 30% 20%;"
          />
        </picture>
        <div>
          <p class="text-sm font-mono text-accent mb-1 tracking-wide">About</p>
          <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-bold text-slate-50">
            Builder, scientist, leader.
          </h1>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="space-y-5 text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          I lead engineering at
          <a
            href="https://ed.stanford.edu/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="text-accent hover:text-accent-light transition-colors"
            >Stanford University</a
          >
          for
          <a
            href="/roar"
            class="text-accent hover:text-accent-light transition-colors"
            >ROAR</a
          >, a K-12 reading-assessment platform deployed in 309 districts and
          2,700+ schools, used by 160K+ students. I built the engineering team
          and own the technical decisions that take ROAR from a research
          platform to a system districts can trust for classroom use. Hiring,
          roadmap, architecture, vendor management, FERPA-grade data governance,
          budget. All of it lives with me.
        </p>
        <p>
          AI is changing what a small engineering team can do. We use coding
          agents, AI code review on every PR, and generative testing on the code
          paths where mistakes would matter most. The work that decides whether
          any of this helps isn't the prompts. It's the documentation
          underneath: ~70 pages of domain knowledge, project conventions, and
          review heuristics that ground every AI-assisted change. Inside the
          product, the focus is psychometric integrity: validated assessments
          built on modern measurement theory (CAT, latent-ability models, IRT
          item banks), with validation work done in partnership with the
          <a
            href="https://acceleratelearning.stanford.edu/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="text-accent hover:text-accent-light transition-colors"
            >Stanford Accelerator for Learning</a
          >
          and published in peer-reviewed journals. Both halves of that get harder
          when the users are children.
        </p>
        <p>
          Before this role I was a postdoc at the
          <a
            href="https://escience.washington.edu/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="text-accent hover:text-accent-light transition-colors"
            >UW eScience Institute</a
          >, where I built open-source tools that ended up in research
          workflows: pyAFQ (Nature Methods), Cloudknot (SciPy), Groupyr (JOSS),
          and AFQ-Insight (PLOS Computational Biology). My PhD is in
          computational physics from the University of Washington, where I ran
          Monte Carlo simulations on DOE supercomputers as a
          <a
            href="https://www.krellinst.org/csgf/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="text-accent hover:text-accent-light transition-colors"
            >Computational Science Graduate Fellow</a
          >
          and published in Physical Review Letters.
        </p>
        <p>
          Before grad school I served as a
          <a
            href="https://www.peacecorps.gov/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="text-accent hover:text-accent-light transition-colors"
            >U.S. Peace Corps</a
          >
          health-education volunteer in Morocco, designing federally funded youth
          programs in rural schools and co-organizing the country's first national
          English spelling bee. Before that I was a U.S. Air Force officer and systems
          engineer at the
          <a
            href="https://www.losangeles.af.mil/About-Us/Fact-Sheets/Article/343702/space-and-missile-systems-center/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="text-accent hover:text-accent-light transition-colors"
            >Space and Missile Systems Center</a
          >, where I program-managed an $8M software platform and helped link a
          $2.4B lab budget with a $12B satellite R&D portfolio.
        </p>
        <p>
          The thread through all of it is an unusual mix: scientific rigor,
          structured engineering, and the slow work of building community. All
          three show up in what I lead now.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-12 pt-10 border-t border-slate-800/50">
        <h2
          class="text-xs font-mono text-slate-400 uppercase tracking-widest mb-6"
        >
          What I Own
        </h2>
        <ul class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-x-8 gap-y-3 text-sm text-slate-300">
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="text-accent mt-0.5">▸</span>
            Engineering strategy, technical roadmap, and operating cadence
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="text-accent mt-0.5">▸</span>
            Hiring, mentorship, and team structure (currently six engineers)
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="text-accent mt-0.5">▸</span>
            AI-accelerated engineering practice — tooling, QA, evaluation
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="text-accent mt-0.5">▸</span>
            End-to-end architecture: web platform, APIs, data pipelines, integrations
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="text-accent mt-0.5">▸</span>
            Reliability discipline: monitoring, incident response, release management
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="text-accent mt-0.5">▸</span>
            Data governance, privacy &amp; user safety on a K-12 platform (FERPA-aligned)
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="text-accent mt-0.5">▸</span>
            Technology budget, vendor selection, and contractor management
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="text-accent mt-0.5">▸</span>
            Cross-functional partnership with Product, Research, and 25+ school districts
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-slate-800/50 py-20">
    <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6">
      <h2
        class="text-xs font-mono text-slate-400 uppercase tracking-widest mb-12"
      >
        How I Work
      </h2>
      <div class="max-w-3xl space-y-8 text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-100 mb-2">
            AI is only as useful as the documentation underneath it
          </h3>
          <p class="text-base text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            On a small team, AI either compounds your output or wastes your
            time. The work that decides which way it goes isn't the prompts.
            It's the domain knowledge, conventions, and review heuristics that
            any engineer or coding agent can learn from before writing a line.
            We've written ~70 pages of that for our dashboard repo alone.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-100 mb-2">
            80% of the value with 20% of the effort
          </h3>
          <p class="text-base text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Building for the future isn't the same as over-engineering for the
            present. I push the team to find the smallest version of the right
            thing and ship it, then earn the right to iterate. Most of the wins
            on the platforms I've led came from saying no to the overbuild.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-100 mb-2">
            Reliability is the cheapest feature you can ship
          </h3>
          <p class="text-base text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Monitoring, audit logs, CI/CD, and a clean on-call rotation don't
            slow a team down. They're how you avoid spending the next quarter
            fighting the fires you should have caught. The setup work pays back
            fast.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-100 mb-2">
            Engineering and Product is a partnership
          </h3>
          <p class="text-base text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            The teams I've been part of that worked best had clear partnership
            between an engineering leader and a product leader. The engineering
            side owns
            <em>how</em>. Product owns <em>what</em> and <em>why</em>. I prefer
            to share accountability for both, and work disagreements out before
            either of us walks into a room of stakeholders.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-100 mb-2">
            Privacy and safety are design constraints, not paperwork
          </h3>
          <p class="text-base text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Working on a platform that serves children raises the floor on what
            safety and privacy mean. Least-privilege access, system-of-record
            clarity, retention and deletion handled in code, vendor review.
            These aren't compliance theater. They're how you avoid being the
            headline.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-slate-800/50 py-20">
    <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6">
      <h2
        class="text-xs font-mono text-slate-400 uppercase tracking-widest mb-12"
      >
        Timeline
      </h2>
      <div class="max-w-2xl">
        <TimelineItem
          year="2023–present"
          title="Director of Technology & Innovation"
          org="Stanford University — ROAR / Graduate School of Education"
          detail="Leading technical development of web-based reading assessments and data infrastructure."
        />
        <TimelineItem
          year="2022–2023"
          title="Research Scientist"
          org="Stanford University — ROAR / Developmental-Behavioral Pediatrics"
          detail="Data science, neuroimaging, and educational technology."
        />
        <TimelineItem
          year="2020–2022"
          title="Data Science Postdoctoral Fellow"
          org="University of Washington — eScience Institute"
          detail="Built open-source tools for reproducible neuroimaging analysis."
        />
        <TimelineItem
          year="2014–2020"
          title="PhD, Nuclear Physics"
          org="University of Washington"
          detail="Computational Science Graduate Fellowship. Quantum Monte Carlo simulations on DOE supercomputers."
        />
        <TimelineItem
          year="2011–2013"
          title="Peace Corps Volunteer"
          org="U.S. Peace Corps — Kingdom of Morocco"
          detail="Health education, community infrastructure, and peer education programs."
        />
        <TimelineItem
          year="2006–2010"
          title="Air Force Officer & Systems Engineer"
          org="U.S. Air Force — Space and Missile Systems Center"
        />
        <TimelineItem
          year="2010"
          title="MS, Condensed Matter Physics"
          org="California State University, Long Beach"
          detail="Graduate Dean's List (top 1.5%)."
        />
        <TimelineItem
          year="2006"
          title="BS, Engineering Physics"
          org="Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University"
          detail="Summa Cum Laude."
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
